import type { CollectionInput, EntityInput, TechnologyInput } from "@/lib/schema";
import type { EvidenceGrade } from "@/lib/complementary";

/**
 * Recovery and rejuvenation in people treated for cancer as children, teenagers or young adults.
 *
 * This is the best-documented evidence base on recovery in all of oncology, and until this file the corpus held
 * almost none of it. Four cohorts are the reason anything here is known: the Childhood Cancer Survivor Study, the
 * St Jude Lifetime Cohort, the British Childhood Cancer Survivor Study and the European PanCareSurFup consortium.
 * They are written as records a reader can open, by name, because the numbers everyone quotes are theirs.
 *
 * Every figure below was read from the abstract or full text of the paper cited beside it and carries its cohort,
 * its age and its denominator, because these figures are misquoted more than any others in survivorship: "two
 * thirds of survivors have a chronic condition" is the Childhood Cancer Survivor Study at a mean age of 26, and
 * "ninety-five per cent" is the St Jude Lifetime Cohort at age 45 using clinical testing rather than self-report.
 * Both are true. They are not the same statement and they do not describe the same population.
 *
 * Children are not small adults in this field. Radiotherapy to a growing brain, a growing skeleton and an
 * immature gonad does things it does not do to an adult, and the late effects that follow are developmental:
 * height not reached, puberty that arrives early or not at all, a reading age that falls behind rather than a
 * skill that is lost, teeth that never formed. The adult records in this front cover the same organs in people
 * treated as adults, and are referenced rather than repeated.
 *
 * Each record carries an honest grade as a tag `evidence:<grade>`, the model of src/data/complementary.ts:
 * strong = consistent evidence plus a major guideline recommendation; moderate = evidence exists but is limited,
 * mixed or single-cohort; insufficient = no reliable human evidence; no-benefit; harm. Here the grade describes
 * the surveillance or intervention the record recommends, not the certainty that the late effect is real.
 *
 * Second cancers in people treated as adults belong to another file in this round; this one holds the paediatric
 * cohort evidence and the screening that follows from it. Transplant and graft-versus-host disease,
 * rehabilitation as a discipline, the mind, and the measurement instruments are likewise elsewhere.
 *
 * British spelling.
 */

